---
import ClubLogo from '../ClubLogo.astro';
import ClubMenu from './ClubMenu.astro';
import { CLUB_BANNER, CLUB_CTA, CLUB_HOME, CLUB_NAV } from '@src/shared/club-nav';

// The club website's header: the notice, the wordmark, the bar and the Menu.
//
// ## ⚠️ One `<header>`, and it is the page's `banner` landmark
//
// This is where the club pages and the money pages deliberately differ in markup, so read
// this before "fixing" the inconsistency against `SiteBanner.astro`.
//
// Over on the money pages the notice is an `<aside class="site-banner">` and the bar is a
// separate `<nav class="site-nav">`, neither inside a `<header>`. That is forced: five
// Nightingale Nightmare pages carry `NnMasthead`, which *is* a `<header>` outside `<main>`
// and therefore the `banner` landmark, and a second one is `landmark-no-duplicate-banner`.
//
// **No club page has a masthead.** So the constraint does not apply here and the cost of
// copying it does — as a bare `<aside>` plus a bare `<nav>`, the welcome sentence sits outside
// the page's landmark structure, which is exactly what axe's `region` rule flags and what
// cost 39 violations the last time this repository got it wrong. One `<header>`, the notice
// inside it, everything in a landmark.
//
// ## Why it is rendered before `<main>`
//
// The skip link points at `#main`. A navigation rendered *inside* `<main>` would put the links
// after the thing the skip link skips to — the one control that exists to jump past the
// navigation would land in front of it. `ClubBase.astro` renders this above `<main>` for that
// reason, and it is also what makes `<header>` map to `banner` at all.
//
// ## The wordmark is the Home link
//
// There is no "Home" item in the bar, which is the one departure from the money pages' nav.
// The mark carries `aria-label="Southville Running Club, home"` so it is announced as what it
// does rather than as three words of artwork, and it takes `aria-current="page"` on `/` —
// without which somebody using a screen reader would be told which section they are in
// everywhere except the home page.
//
// ## The banner sentence is written wall to wall, on purpose
//
// ⚠️ Astro compresses the newline between a tag and the text after it to **nothing**, so any
// line break inside the sentence below silently swallows the space before "the old site" or
// the one before "while". `CLUB_BANNER` is split into `lead` / `linkLabel` / `tail` precisely
// so the join carries its own spacing; do not reflow these lines to satisfy a formatter.

const pathname = Astro.url.pathname;
---

<header class="club-header">
  <div class="club-banner">
    <div class="club-wrap">
      <p>
        <strong>{CLUB_BANNER.welcome}</strong>
        <span
          >{CLUB_BANNER.lead}<a href={CLUB_BANNER.clubWebsite}>{CLUB_BANNER.linkLabel}</a
          >{CLUB_BANNER.tail}</span
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="club-wrap club-header-inner">
    <a
      class="club-mark"
      href={CLUB_HOME.href}
      aria-label={CLUB_HOME.label}
      aria-current={CLUB_HOME.match.test(pathname) ? 'page' : undefined}
    >
      {
        /* `labelled={false}`, because the link's own `aria-label` already says the club's
        name — announcing it twice in a row helps nobody. */
      }
      <ClubLogo class="club-logo" width={132} labelled={false} />
    </a>

    <nav class="club-nav" aria-label="Southville Running Club">
      <ul>
        {
          CLUB_NAV.map(({ href, label, match }) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href} aria-current={match.test(pathname) ? 'page' : undefined}>
                {label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <a class="club-btn club-btn-primary club-cta" href={CLUB_CTA.href}>{CLUB_CTA.label}</a
    >

    <ClubMenu />
  </div>
</header>
